.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_NEW_LISTENER 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_new_listener ,
.Nm SSL_new_listener_from ,
.Nm SSL_is_listener ,
.Nm SSL_get0_listener ,
.Nm SSL_listen ,
.Nm SSL_accept_connection ,
.Nm SSL_get_accept_connection_queue_len ,
.Nm SSL_new_from_listener ,
.Nm SSL_ACCEPT_CONNECTION_NO_BLOCK
.Nd SSL object interface for abstracted connection acceptance
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_new_listener
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "uint64_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_new_listener_from
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "uint64_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_is_listener
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_get0_listener
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_listen
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Fd #define SSL_ACCEPT_CONNECTION_NO_BLOCK
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_accept_connection
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "uint64_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft size_t
.Fo SSL_get_accept_connection_queue_len
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_new_from_listener
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "uint64_t flags"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn SSL_new_listener
function creates a listener SSL object.
Listener SSL objects are specialised to only accept network connections
in a protocol- agnostic manner.
They cannot be used, for example, for sending or receiving data using
.Xr SSL_write_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3 .
In general, only those functions expressly documented as being supported
on a listener SSL object are available.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_new_listener_from
function creates a listener SSL object which is subordinate to a QUIC
domain SSL object
.Fa ssl .
See
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3
and
.Xr openssl-quic-concurrency 7
for details on QUIC domain SSL objects.
.Pp
A listener SSL object supports the following operations:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Standard reference counting and free operations, such as
.Xr SSL_up_ref 3
and
.Xr SSL_free 3 ;
.It
Network BIO configuration operations, such as
.Xr SSL_set_bio 3 ;
.It
Event processing and polling enablement APIs such as
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_wpoll_descriptor 3 ,
.Xr SSL_net_read_desired 3
and
.Xr SSL_net_write_desired 3 ;
.It
Certain configurable parameters described in
.Xr SSL_get_value_uint 3
(see
.Xr SSL_get_value_uint 3
for details);
.It
Accepting network connections using the functions documented in this
manual page, such as
.Fn SSL_accept_connection .
.El
.Pp
The basic workflow of using a listener object is as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Create a new listener object using
.Fn SSL_new_listener
using a
.Vt SSL_CTX
which uses a supported
.Sy SSL_METHOD
(such as
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3 Ns );
.It
Configure appropriate network BIOs using
.Xr SSL_set_bio 3
on the listener SSL object;
.It
Configure the blocking mode using
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 ;
.It
Accept connections in a loop by calling
.Fn SSL_accept_connection .
Each returned SSL object is a valid connection which can be used in a
normal manner.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_is_listener
function returns 1 if and only if a SSL object is a listener SSL object.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_get0_listener
function returns a listener object which is related to the given SSL
object, if there is one.
For a listener object, this is the same object (the function returns its
argument).
For a connection object which was created by a listener object, that
listener object is returned.
If the
.Fa ssl
argument is an SSL object which is not a listener object and which is
not descended from a listener object (e.g.
a connection obtained using
.Fn SSL_accept_connection )
or indirectly from a listener object (e.g.
a QUIC stream SSL object obtained using
.Xr SSL_accept_stream 3
called on a connection obtained using
.Fn SSL_accept_connection )
the return value is NULL. See NOTES below for caveats related to pending
SSL connections on a QUIC listener's accept queue.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_listen
function begins monitoring the listener
.Fa ssl
for incoming connections.
Appropriate BIOs must have been configured before calling
.Fn SSL_listen ,
along with any other needed configuration for the listener SSL object.
It is typically not necessary to call
.Fn SSL_listen
because it will be called automatically on the first call to
.Fn SSL_accept_connection .
However,
.Fn SSL_listen
may be called explicitly if it is desired to control precisely when the
listening process begins, or to ensure that no errors occur when
starting to listen for connections.
After a call to
.Fn SSL_listen
(or
.Fn SSL_accept_connection )
succeeds.
The
.Fn SSL_listen
function is idempotent, subsequent calls on the same
.Fa ssl
object are no-ops.
This call is supported only on listener SSL objects.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_accept_connection
call is supported only on a listener SSL object and accepts a new
incoming connection.
A new SSL object representing the accepted connection is created and
returned on success.
If no incoming connection is available and the listener SSL object is
configured in nonblocking mode, NULL is returned.
.Pp
The new SSL object returned from
.Fn SSL_accept_connection
may or may not have completed its handshake at the point it is returned.
Optionally, you may use the function
.Xr SSL_is_init_finished 3
to determine this.
You may call the functions
.Xr SSL_accept 3 ,
.Xr SSL_do_handshake 3
or
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3
to progress the state of the SSL object towards handshake completion.
Other "I/O" functions may also implicitly progress the state of the
handshake such as
.Xr SSL_poll 3 ,
.Xr SSL_read 3
and
.Xr SSL_write 3 .
.Pp
The
.Sy SSL_ACCEPT_CONNECTION_NO_BLOCK
flag may be specified to
.Fn SSL_accept_connection .
If specified, the call does not block even if the listener SSL object is
configured in blocking mode.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_get_accept_connection_queue_len
call returns the number of pending connections on the
.Fa ssl
listener's queue.
.Fn SSL_accept_connection
returns the next pending connection, removing it from the queue.
The returned connection count is a point-in-time value, the actual
number of connections that will ultimately be returned may be different.
.Pp
Currently, listener SSL objects are only supported for QUIC server usage
via
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3 ,
or QUIC client-only usage via
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_method 3
or
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
(see
.Sx CLIENT-ONLY USAGE Ns ).
It is expected that the listener interface, which provides an abstracted
API for connection acceptance, will be expanded to support other
protocols, such as TLS over TCP, plain TCP or DTLS in future.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_listen
and
.Fn SSL_accept_connection
are "I/O" functions, meaning that they update the value returned by
.Xr SSL_get_error 3
if they fail.
.Sh CLIENT-ONLY USAGE
It is also possible to use the listener interface without accepting any
connections and without listening for connections.
This can be useful in circumstances where it is desirable for multiple
connections to share the same underlying network resources.
For example, multiple outgoing QUIC client connections could be made to
use the same underlying UDP socket.
.Pp
To disable client address validation on a listener SSL object, the flag
.Sy SSL_LISTENER_FLAG_NO_VALIDATE
may be passed in the flags field of both
.Fn SSL_new_listener
and
.Fn SSL_new_listener_from .
Note that this flag only impacts the sending of retry frames for server
address validation.
Tokens may still be communicated from the server via NEW_TOKEN frames,
which will still be validated on receipt in future connections.
Note that this setting is not recommended and may be dangerous in
untrusted environments.
Not performing address validation exposes the server to malicious
clients that may open large numbers of connections and never transact
data on them (roughly equivalent to a TCP syn flood attack), which
address validation mitigates.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_new_from_listener
function creates a client connection under a given listener SSL object.
For QUIC, it is also possible to use
.Fn SSL_new_from_listener ,
leading to a UDP network endpoint which has both incoming and outgoing
connections.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument of
.Fn SSL_new_from_listener
is reserved and must be set to 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_new_listener
and
.Fn SSL_new_listener_from
return a new listener SSL object or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_is_listener
returns 1 if its
.Fa ssl
argument is a listener object, 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get0_listener
returns an SSL object pointer (potentially to the same object on which
it is called) or NULL.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_listen
returns 1 on success or 0 on failure.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_accept_connection
returns a pointer to a new SSL object on success or NULL on failure.
On success, the caller assumes ownership of the reference.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get_accept_connection_queue_len
returns a nonnegative value, or 0 if the queue is empty, or called on an
unsupported SSL object type.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_new_from_listener
returns a pointer to a new SSL object on success or NULL on failure.
On success, the caller assumes ownership of the reference.
.Sh NOTES
.Fn SSL_get0_listener
behaves somewhat differently in SSL callbacks for QUIC connections.
As QUIC connections begin TLS handshake operations prior to them being
accepted via
.Fn SSL_accept_connection ,
an application may receive callbacks for such pending connection prior
to acceptance via
.Fn SSL_accept_connection .
As listener association takes place during the accept process, prior to
being returned from
.Fn SSL_accept_connection ,
calls to
.Fn SSL_get0_listener
made from such SSL callbacks will return NULL. This can be used as an
indicator within the callback that the referenced SSL object has not yet
been accepted.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3 ,
.Xr SSL_free 3 ,
.Xr SSL_set_bio 3 ,
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_rpoll_descriptor 3 ,
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3
